#: build/help.js:1
msgid "How does Captain Media Shield optimize images?"
msgstr ""

#: build/help.js:1
msgid "It uses high-performance on-server algorithms (GD / Imagick) to compress images and generate modern WebP and AVIF formats without relying on external cloud APIs or third-party paid subscriptions."
msgstr ""

#: build/help.js:1
msgid "Will converting to WebP/AVIF break my existing image URLs?"
msgstr ""

#: build/help.js:1
msgid "No! Your original image URLs remain completely intact. Captain Media Shield serves Next Generation WebP/AVIF automatically via .htaccess rewrite rules or <picture> tags for browsers that support them."
msgstr ""

#: build/help.js:1
msgid "Can I adjust compression quality for specific images?"
msgstr ""

#: build/help.js:1
msgid "Yes! In addition to global settings (default 80%), you can adjust compression quality per image directly from the Media Library list view (using the Quick Quality popup) and the single attachment details screen."
msgstr ""

#: build/help.js:1
msgid "Where are original backup images stored and how do I restore?"
msgstr ""

#: build/help.js:1
msgid "Original uncompressed files are safely backed up in your wp-content/uploads/capms-backups/ folder. You can click \"Restore\" on any image to instantly bring back the original and remove generated Next Generation files."
msgstr ""

#: build/help.js:1
msgid "Does Captain Media Shield support video compression?"
msgstr ""

#: build/help.js:1
msgid "Yes! It includes FastStart streaming optimization (moving the MP4 moov atom to the front for instant video playback) and optional WebM conversion with VP9/Opus encoding via on-server ffmpeg."
msgstr ""

#: build/help.js:1
msgid "Does Captain Media Shield send my media to external servers?"
msgstr ""

#: build/help.js:1
msgid "Never. 100% of the optimization, conversion, and cleanup takes place directly on your own WordPress host server, ensuring zero data leakage, zero monthly quotas, and complete privacy."
msgstr ""

